{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791700234,"nonce":"cSDdUOuGfEYzzjoftaavpQGi78Q=","title":"Layout animations are not seen in a window when a transition starts in the same frame","message":"In the wiki window a switch's knob jumped to its end on a click and only moved when the mouse moved. Since layout animations are measured at a window's frame (Layout.settleMotion), the measuring call was written `this.#motion ||= this.#native.settleMotion()`: when the frame's restyle had started a transition (the track's colour), `#motion` was already true and the native measure never ran, so the knob's move was never seen. The next layout that started no transition measured it, which a mouse move caused.\n\nA second gap of the same kind: `element.animationsFinished()` asked straight after a change, before the frame that lays it out, found nothing running and resolved, since a layout animation starts at the layout. A panel's exit removed its content before it could be seen folding.\n\nReproduced offscreen with FrameLayout frames and in a real window with captureFrames (knob x per presented frame: 25.7 then 9.8, no frames between).\n\nFix: settleMotion always measures; motionFinished on a node with a layout animation, while a layout change is unsettled, waits for the next settle before deciding.\n\nTests: tests/native-frame.mjs (a knob slides while its track's colour eases; animationsFinished asked before the frame waits for the move). Both fail against the old code.","files":null}]}